<body>
  <header>
    <h1>Colour Blindness Awareness</h1>
    <p>Learn how colour blindness works, what causes it, and how to make design more inclusive.</p>
  </header>
  <main>
    <section>
      <h2>What is Colour Blindness?</h2>
      <p>Colour blindness is a condition that changes the way a person sees colours. It often affects red, green or blue perception, and it usually begins at birth.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Why it Matters</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>1 in 12 men and 1 in 200 women have some form of colour vision difference.</li>
        <li>Accessible design helps people read information without relying only on colour.</li>
        <li>Good contrast, labels, and symbols make websites easier for everyone.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Six Pages of Useful Information</h2>
      <div class="card-grid">
        <div class="card"><strong>1. Causes</strong><p>Learn why colour vision differences happen.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><strong>2. Types</strong><p>Discover the different forms of colour blindness.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><strong>3. Living With It</strong><p>Practical tips for school, work, and everyday life.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><strong>4. Tools</strong><p>Apps and resources that help with colour accessibility.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><strong>5. Awareness</strong><p>How to support people with colour blindness.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><strong>6. Design</strong><p>Best practices for usable websites and visuals.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Next Steps</h2>
      <p>Use this page as a starting point, then create separate pages for each topic when you are ready. The file currently contains the main site content in one place.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
  <footer>
    <p>Colour blindness awareness website — information for designers, educators, and learners.</p>
  </footer>
